1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:06,560
Hi everyone. In today's video, we will perform unit testing using GTest.

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:19,700
First, I have a list of C files and items that need to be tested using GTest.

3
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:25,440
Here, I will select the first item to demonstrate how to perform UT.

4
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:33,910
To be able to write GTest test cases, we first need to create a test environment for this item.

5
00:00:33,910 --> 00:00:38,830
Currently, there are two ways to do this.

6
00:00:38,830 --> 00:00:42,680
The first way is the manual method.

7
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:52,500
We need to prepare a folder with the same name as the item to be tested, and this folder must contain the corresponding C file.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:10,500
Then we drag and drop that C file into the script of cook-san (the .bat file).

9
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:20,470
After that, the tool will automatically analyze it and generate a test environment with all required inputs,

10
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:30,470
including the header files and the C++ file containing our test script.

11
00:01:33,470 --> 00:01:46,230
The second way is to use the tool.

12
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:58,420
We use the tool by filling in the required fields, such as the database path, workspace path, and the items that need to be tested.

13
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:21,700
After that, a complete message will appear, and a test environment similar to the manual method will be generated.

14
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:30,700
It will also contains all the required files.

15
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:42,360
After the environment has been created successfully, using either of the two methods above,

16
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:59,620
we will have all the input headers, the main header file, and the C++ file containing the stub functions, mock functions, and test case design.

17
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:08,390
However, a very common issue when working with UT using GTest

18
00:03:08,390 --> 00:03:16,090
is compiler errors caused by missing definitions of data types, structs, enums,

19
00:03:16,090 --> 00:03:25,160
or related headers, such as the console-related headers shown here.

20
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:32,480
The solution is to use a second tool,

21
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:40,820
which is the UT AI Copilot tool.

22
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:59,820
We need to provide the paths of the item in need of testing, the database path with definition.

23
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:11,360
Then we click Auto Define Object.

24
00:04:55,360 --> 00:05:01,500
The tool has notified SUCCESS,

25
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:20,040
so instead of manually searching for each header file inside the database, we only need to provide some required information.

26
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:38,660
Then the UT AI Copilot tool will automatically search through the database, find the correct definitions,

27
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:50,240
and insert the required information, data types, or definition into the header file.

28
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:58,240
After solving the data-related issues, we will build it again to check whether there are still any errors.

29
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:04,730
We can do this by selecting Terminal, choose Run Task and Build

30
00:06:08,710 --> 00:06:14,610
As you can see, the missing declaration errors are no longer shown.

32
00:06:14,610 --> 00:06:23,390
Instead, we now have undefined references for some external functions.

34
00:06:23,390 --> 00:06:35,170
This is a sign that these external functions need to be stubbed or mocked to isolate the scope of the module we are testing.

36
00:06:35,170 --> 00:06:50,060
To handle the stub and mock generation of these functions, we can use the Prepair and Get SUTs function in the AI Copilot tool.

37
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:14,400
The tool has finished running. It has added two functions and mocked them.

38
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:35,290
Now we will build it again to check whether the previous undefined reference errors still exist.

40
00:07:39,610 --> 00:07:42,770
Now there are no more undefined reference errors.

41
00:07:42,770 --> 00:07:54,190
Instead, the sample test case shown below has been executed successfully.

42
00:07:54,190 --> 00:07:59,960
Next, we will design a test case.

43
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,000
After the issue with undefined reference functions has been resolved

44
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
by stubbing and mocking those functions.

45
00:08:11,190 --> 00:08:15,130
The next step is to design a test case.

46
00:08:15,130 --> 00:08:20,940
Here, I have a sample test case.

47
00:08:24,350 --> 00:08:31,900
A standard test case usually consists of six parts.

47
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:35,900
The first part is the macro test.

48
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:42,530
Inside it, there will be the test fixture and the test case name.

49
00:08:42,530 --> 00:08:55,000
The purpose of the test fixture is to group related test cases together, while the test case name is used to identify each test case.

50
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,740
The second part is the description.

50
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:04,650
Here, we describe the testing purpose, such as which logic is being checked,

51
00:09:04,650 --> 00:09:23,480
what special input is used, or whether it covers the true or false branch of a certain condition.

52
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:29,500
The third part is the input initialize section.

53
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:46,900
This is the preparation step, where we declare variables and assign the required input values into the tested function.

54
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:53,300
The fourth part is the call stub function section.

55
00:09:53,300 --> 00:10:04,850
If our function goes through sub-functions that were stubbed in the previous step,

56
00:10:08,850 --> 00:10:25,060
we need to set up the return values or call those functions according to the code flow.

57
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:38,060
The fifth part is the call SUTs, where we call the function that we need to test.

58
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:51,300
Finally, we use EXPECT_EQ or other check functions to compare the actual result with the expected value.

59
00:10:51,300 --> 00:11:00,000
If they match, the result will be Pass.

59
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:11,590
If the check result does not match, the result will be Fail.

60
00:11:11,590 --> 00:11:33,070
Now we will try building it, and there are a few errors here. 

61
00:11:35,070 --> 00:11:46,440
The function I am calling here, which is "Security Level", is currently declared as a void type.

62
00:11:46,440 --> 00:12:00,000
But it has a return statement, so I will remove this return value.

63
00:12:16,530 --> 00:12:33,760
The first test case has passed, and my check expected variables are correct.

64
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:50,540
However, we need to know how much percentage of the function logic this test case has covered.

65
00:12:50,540 --> 00:13:05,500
To answer this question, we will move to the next step, which is building the Cantata coverage

66
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:25,540
And we will use a third tool called "Cantata IDE".

67
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:47,330
We will add the Cantata folder that we had just built in order to view the coverage percentage.

68
00:13:55,330 --> 00:14:16,260
As you can see, this test case is currently designed to cover the true case of this condition which has been covered correctly.

69
00:14:39,260 --> 00:14:44,750
I will add one more test case.

70
00:14:44,750 --> 00:14:55,360
This test case will cover the false case of this condition at line 59 and check the coverage.

71
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:52,140
The second test case has also passed. 

71
00:15:52,140 --> 00:16:06,000
We will check the coverage once again, and the false condition has been covered.

72
00:16:12,140 --> 00:16:17,070
Similarly, for the remaining conditions,

73
00:16:17,070 --> 00:17:03,300
we need to add more test cases to cover each condition to achieve 100% coverage.

74
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:20,880
I have just added the prepared test cases, and all test cases have passed. 

74
00:17:20,880 --> 00:17:30,000
All 14 test cases passed successfully and achieved 100% coverage.

75
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Now we will check the Cantata coverage again. 

75
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:42,000
All conditions have been fully covered.

76
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:54,000
After the test cases have achieved 100% coverage,

77
00:17:54,000 --> 00:18:10,000
we will submit them for review and continue with the next UT steps, such as Review, Deliver and PDC.

78
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:17,000
I will end this video of how to perform UT using GTest here.

79
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:21,000
Thank you for listening.
